# Catalog scripts must have LF line endings on every platform, including Windows.
#
# This is not a style preference. Windows entries are executed through Git Bash (see
# exec/shell-resolution.ts), and bash reading a CRLF script treats the trailing \r as part of the
# last token on every line: `set -e\r` becomes an unknown command, and a quoted string spanning a
# line break silently gains a carriage return. The failure mode is `$'\r': command not found`,
# which names neither the file nor the real cause.
#
# Git for Windows sets core.autocrlf=true by default, so without this file a clone on Windows —
# including a GitHub Actions windows-latest checkout — converts all 170 entries' scripts to CRLF
# and every one of them breaks.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary files git must never touch.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.ico binary
